--- MESSAGE ---
DomainLine is the first transform type, and join is the first declared symbol

Dev's shape for a transform type — a substitution string with a mapping factory, string literals
interleaved with calls — has its first instance:

    type DomainLine from Surface
      factory
        parameters
          par package [String]
          par name String
        body
          fix value String "domain " ++ [ join parameters
            par " "
            par _package
          ] ++ " " ++ _name
    domain draco draketarget DrakeTarget

Two things moved from the sketch, both said before building. The two lambda helpers dropped out:
the parser still carries a lambda as opaque text (#61), so authoring them would have put drake-form
text into the Scala verbatim, and one was the identity anyway. And the join of the package words
became a DECLARED NAME rather than a Scala atom: `mkString` in a target-side type would be the
residue that stops a Haskell framework binary from ever rendering the same definition. Dev asked
whether `join` or `splice` reads better, and whether an invented infix such as `+|+` would work.
`join`: `splice` means insertion in JavaScript and Perl, and an invented operator carries no meaning
in until learned and costs a reservation and a fixity entry, where a name uses the application
surface that exists. Separator first, Haskell's and Java's order.

THE SYMBOL SEAM. `join` is applied by name on the surface and spelled per target: DracoGenerator's
"()" case now consults a one-row table before rendering an ordinary call. That table is the seam
GenScala's transform types will own; today it is the smallest thing that lets a neutral name reach
Scala.

DRAKE-FIRST, LITERALLY. The .drake was written first; DrakeCLI parse produced the .json, and it is
byte-identical in style to the hand-shaped corpus — the first time a definition entered the tree
through the parser rather than through JSON. The emitter reproduces the authored text exactly. The
.scala came from the GeneratorCLI with one line spliced, because the compiled engine predates the
symbol table; that line was then compiled and run verbatim under 2.13.16 outside the project, and
printed the domain line. `++` on String is known good before the suite runs.

THE FIRST RUN. 589 / 11 — eleven failures with one cause: the symbol table was an eager val in an
object that extends App, so it was null until main, and every application rendering in the suite
fell over it. DRACO.md's first rule names companions; the engine object binds to it just the same,
and the rule now says so.

THE ORACLE. DomainLineTest builds a DomainLine from every src/main definition's own domain pointer
and asserts it equals the committed .drake's `domain` line. The one parameterized domain line in
the corpus, Format(F), is outside the shape — a name is a String — and is counted, not compared.
The mapping from a definition to the two arguments is done by the test today; making it a
transform type in GenDrake is the next increment, and this test is already its oracle.

